## Overview

This project investigates **Procedural Content Generation (PCG)** techniques in Unity, with a particular focus on whether procedurally generated environments can produce visually appealing scenes. Players explore infinite biome-based terrain, capture in-game photographs, and receive automatic aesthetic quality scores based on established compositional principles.

---

## Features

### Procedural Terrain Generation
- Infinite, chunk-based terrain built with multi-octave Perlin noise
- Multiple configurable biomes, each with unique textures, colours, and audio
- Height-based terrain colouring (sand, grass, rock, snow)
- Level of Detail (LOD) system for efficient rendering at distance
- Threaded map and mesh generation to keep the main thread responsive

### Biome System
- Select a biome from the main menu before entering the world
- Each biome defines its own noise parameters, colour regions, ambient sounds, wind effects, and optional ocean
- Examples include Desert and Village-themed environments

### Vegetation
- Procedurally placed trees using seeded RNG for deterministic, reproducible placement
- GPU instancing via `DrawMeshInstanced` — up to 1023 trees rendered per batch
- Physics colliders active only at close range (LOD 0) to preserve performance

### Dynamic Environment
- Day/night cycle with sun and moon movement
- Bird spawning for atmospheric depth
- Biome-specific wind particle effects
- Context-aware footstep audio (sand, grass, snow)

### Photography Mode
- In-game camera with UI overlay and shutter feedback
- Rule-of-thirds grid overlay (toggle with **G**)
- Automatic aesthetic scoring on every captured photo
- Gallery to browse all saved photos with their scores and metadata

